# ComposeUX — The Mode-Nav Tier: Selection-Bound Navigation Above `cic_menu`'s Screen Grid [SPEC:CXC-MODE-NAV]

> Extends [SPEC:CXC-MENU] into the tier it named and explicitly scoped itself out of: the mode row (STD/DRADIS/EXT/CHRONO) and each mode's own navigation, folded into ONE tree ([CXC-MENU-SCHEMA]) — David ratified the fold NOW, not later (audit round 1, Q1). A generic `selection` primitive backs every nav level; a soft selector and a physical control (a 7-position rotary, a keypad) are CO-EQUAL PEERS publishing the SAME station-scoped namespace, never a locally-toggled soft button (Q2: confirmed purely bus-derived). The selector EXTENDS [CXC-CIC-TABROW] — not a parallel widget; the namespace EXTENDS [CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION] — not a parallel station model. Every screen still resolves UNIFORMLY as a `screen` leaf ([CXC-MENU-KINDS]) — no new node-kind. BSG's Colonial Console is instance #1: DRADIS/EXT are PRIOR-BUILT worked examples running on (a private host name, not published) (C1); CHRONO's 3-option nav (SHIP TIME / JUMP COUNT / TIMER, Q3) is the first worked case of a mode with its own nav beneath it, three screen-leaves sharing the already-built `colonial_clock` widget (C4). `[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]` — filed as a NEW numbered doc (`78`, the next free prefix after [SPEC:CXC-MENU]'s `77`) following [DD-MENU-SPEC-HOME]'s precedent, pending the architect's own placement ruling. **Composed from the `cic-mode-nav` design session (2026-08-18); reworked per the independent audit's ratified answers (`docs/architecture/AUDIT-cic-mode-nav.md`, [CXC-MODE-NAV-AUDIT-R1]). Two forks remain OPEN: [DD-MODE-NAV-RADIO-NAMESPACE-RENAME], [DD-MODE-NAV-CLOCK-WIDGET-REGISTRATION].**

---

## 1. Intent & the tier this fills

### [CXC-MODE-NAV-WHY] The tier [SPEC:CXC-MENU] named and scoped itself out of

- definition: [SPEC:CXC-MENU]'s own instance node draws the line explicitly: *"Mode selection and mode-surface resolution are a DIFFERENT, SHALLOWER tier this resolver instance does NOT model: a mode-surface file resolves by a suffix convention (`<slug>_mode.svg`), not by the directory-join convention [CXC-MENU-PATH] derives, so folding a mode into THIS tree as a leaf would derive the WRONG path"* ([CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE], its `corrected` note). This spec is that adjacent tier — not a second engine, the SAME engine ([CXC-MENU-SCHEMA] through [CXC-MENU-LINT]) applied to a tree that now carries the mode level too, reconciled with the suffix-convention mismatch by [CXC-MENU-ANNOTATION]'s existing `path` override rather than by any new mechanism ([CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-INSTANCE]).
- fold-is-ratified: the audit's own headline finding was that the architect had asked David "now or later?" on folding the mode row into the tree and **David never answered it in the original session** — this document's tree was built on the unanswered half. Audit round 1, Q1, closed it: *"Do you want the mode row folded into `cic_menu` as the top tree level NOW..., or is that a LATER consolidation...?"* — **David's ratify: "YES."** [CXC-MODE-NAV-TREE]'s status is now `decided`, not `proposed`.
- role: names the seam per [DX-ENGINE-VS-POLICY] the same way [CXC-MENU-WHY] does one tier down — the GENERIC mechanism (the `selection` primitive: namespace-bound, pick-one-of-N, subscribe+publish) is engine content (sections 2-4 below) and ports to any ComposeIO app with a nav tier; BSG's 4 modes, their labels, and the ship-clock art are POLICY (section 6).
- requirement: sections 2-4 ([CXC-MODE-NAV-SELECTION] through [CXC-MODE-NAV-RESOLVER-SEAM]) MUST NOT reference a BSG-specific mode name, an SVG element name, or the `slot:@` grammar in their DEFINITIONS (worked examples may cite BSG for concreteness, matching [CXC-MENU-WHY]'s own practice) — the primitive must be readable by an app author who has never heard of Colonial Console.
- see-also: [DX-ENGINE-VS-POLICY], [CXC-MENU-WHY], [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE], [CXC-MODE-NAV-AUDIT-R1]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MODE-NAV]

---

## 2. The `selection` primitive

### [CXC-MODE-NAV-SELECTION] The `selection` primitive — pick-one-of-N, bound to a namespace, control-is-a-peer

- definition: a SELECTION is the generic shape every nav level in the mode-nav tree realizes — a pick-one-of-N choice, published as an `{index, key}` payload to a namespace, with NO privileged input source. This is not a new mechanism: it is the EXISTING pattern [CXC-CIC-CREDO] and [CXC-CIC-TABROW] already implement for the console/sub-tab tab rows, named here as the GENERAL primitive so a mode row, a mode's own sub-nav (CHRONO's 3 options), and any FUTURE nav level are the same thing wearing different labels, not N different bespoke mechanisms.
- requirement: a selection MUST publish to exactly one station-scoped namespace ([CXC-MODE-NAV-STATION-SCOPE]); EVERY realization of that selection — a soft selector widget, a physical rotary/keypad, an Intelligence — MUST publish the SAME `{index, key}` shape to the SAME namespace and MUST be indistinguishable to any subscriber, generalizing [CXC-CIC-CREDO]'s control-is-a-peer rule from the two levels it already covers (console, sub-tab) to every nav level this tree defines; a soft selector widget MUST derive its displayed selected state FROM the published namespace value and MUST NOT hold or display any locally-toggled selection state of its own — this generalizes [CXC-CIC-TABROW]'s existing per-widget requirement ("the selected indicator MUST be DERIVED from the published index, not local tap-state guesswork") from its two current instances (`row2`, `row3`) to every selector this tree mounts, and is the same invariant [DX-STATE-ON-BUS] states platform-wide.
- on-soft-buttons-with-state: RATIFIED reading (audit round 1, Q2). David: *"we may want to have the UX 'soft buttons' have different states that they maintain"* (design session, 2026-08-18) was audited as ambiguous between (a) purely bus-derived display state (this node's reading) and (b) a genuinely stateful control. **David's ratify: "(a) — a purely bus-derived display state."** His own elaboration, verbatim: *"The pcb in ~/maker/pcb/button_matrix_4x4 is a good physical example of what we want to be able to do with virtual buttons. The Galactica software object (compose-bus resident) will maintain state about all of its subsystems. The state of these subsystems will be published. Different button groups will react in different ways to different semantic messages. When the FTL engine is ready, a big red button labeled 'JUMP READY' may start to blink on screen and in real life (different subscribers)."* (audit round 1, Q2, 2026-08-18) — this is the SAME invariant this node already states (state lives on the bus; a subscriber, soft or physical, renders it), now confirmed by David rather than inferred through [DX-STATE-ON-BUS] alone. This confirmed model is the evidentiary basis for [CXC-MODE-NAV-DEFERRED]'s control/display taxonomy note — see there for the "different control" this spec still does not build.
- see-also: [CXC-CIC-CREDO], [CXC-CIC-TABROW], [DX-STATE-ON-BUS], [CXC-MODE-NAV-STATION-SCOPE], [CXC-MODE-NAV-SELECTOR-SKIN], [CXC-MODE-NAV-DEFERRED]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MODE-NAV]

---

## 3. The selector — extending CXC-CIC-TABROW, not a parallel abstraction

### [CXC-MODE-NAV-SELECTOR-SKIN] The mode-nav selector IS [CXC-CIC-TABROW], instanced further — not a new widget

- definition: RECONCILED per audit (the "biggest correction"). [CXC-CIC-TABROW] already states the exact model this node used to assert as new: *"both CIC tab rows (`row2-tabs` consoles, `row3-tabs` sub-tabs) are ONE generalized tab-row widget, INSTANCED twice — NOT two separate widgets (generalize-don't-fork)... parameterized by (a) the LABEL SET / text bindings, (b) the SELECTED-INDICATOR STYLE, and (c) the indicator FILL color+opacity"* — with `selected_indicator ∈ {filled-background, pill-overlay}` as its existing style axis. The mode row and CHRONO's 3-option nav are a THIRD and FOURTH instancing of that SAME widget — a new label set (the mode names / SHIP TIME-JUMP COUNT-TIMER) and a station-scoped publish namespace ([CXC-MODE-NAV-STATION-SCOPE]) bound the SAME way `row2`/`row3` already are — not a new "selector" widget kind, and not a fork of [CXC-COMP-WIDGET-KIND]'s registered set.
- requirement: a mode-nav nav level's selector MUST be built by extending [CXC-CIC-TABROW] (a new instancing: label set + publish namespace + a style token, reusing `filled-background`/`pill-overlay` or registering a further style value on the SAME parameter) — an author MUST NOT introduce a separate "selector" widget class, and MUST NOT amend [CXC-COMP-WIDGET-KIND]'s registered `radio`/`tab_row` slot-grammar tokens to accomplish this (that is a DIFFERENT composition layer — a per-screen `slot:` binding — that nothing in the mode-nav tier's own chrome-level selectors touches; see [DD-MODE-NAV-SELECTOR-KIND-CONSOLIDATION], now closed).
- see-also: [CXC-CIC-TABROW], [CXC-CIC-NAV], [CXC-COMP-WIDGET-KIND], [CXC-MODE-NAV-SELECTION], [DD-MODE-NAV-SELECTOR-KIND-CONSOLIDATION]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MODE-NAV]

---

## 4. STATION scope — extending CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION, not a parallel model

### [CXC-MODE-NAV-STATION-SCOPE] STATION means exactly what [CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION] already defines; this node only appends namespace levels

- definition: RECONCILED per audit (the "biggest correction"). [CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION] already defines a duty station in full — "a display + its physical controls, operated as one CIC position," a dotted `location.instance.role` id shape (e.g. `cic.1.helm`), and a station-scoped namespace convention (`colonial_console/<station>/console`, `/subtab`, `/status`, `/focus/<domain>`, `/identity`, `/peripherals/<device_id>`). This node mints NO new station concept. Its entire content is: (1) two ADDITIONAL entries for that SAME namespace list — `colonial_console/<station>/mode` (the mode row's selection) and `colonial_console/<station>/chrono` (CHRONO's 3-option nav's selection), following the exact pattern `console`/`subtab` already set; and (2) David's design-session elaboration of the `role` segment already present in [CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION]'s station-id shape, recorded here because it grounds that segment's LOOSE-coupling semantics in a concrete example.
- reconciliation: [CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION]'s station-id shape is already `location.instance.role` — `cic.1.helm` reads location=`cic`, instance=`1`, role=`helm`. David's remark is a real-world WORKED EXAMPLE of exactly that `role` segment: the physical 7-position rotary is wired to a station, and which role the station plays (helm / FTL / DC) is a CONFIGURATION fact about that station, not a hardwired identity of the rotary or the player. Nothing here amends [CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION]'s shape; it is the shape's own worked instance, now on the graph.
- requirement: a mode-nav selection MUST carry the `<station>` scope segment exactly as [CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION] already requires for every CIC namespace, publishing under `colonial_console/<station>/mode` and `colonial_console/<station>/chrono` alongside the existing `console`/`subtab`/`status`/`focus`/`identity`/`peripherals` entries — an app author MUST treat these as two more lines in [CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION]'s ONE list, not a second list; and MUST NOT encode a role or player identity anywhere but the station id's own `role` segment — player count and identity stay dynamic and outside every one of these namespaces, consistent with the loose-coupling reconciliation above.
- namespace-delimiter: DECIDED (audit round 1, Q4) — `/`, matching [CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION] exactly, no dual support. See [DD-MODE-NAV-NAMESPACE-CONVENTION], now closed.
- see-also: [CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION], [CXC-CIC-ROTARY-HOOKUP], [CXC-CIC-NAV], [DD-MODE-NAV-NAMESPACE-CONVENTION]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MODE-NAV]

