## 5. The mode-nav tree

### [CXC-MODE-NAV-TREE] The tree gains a mode level at the top: mode -> per-mode nav -> leaves — RATIFIED

- definition: the SAME [CXC-MENU-SCHEMA] tree ([SPEC:CXC-MENU] §2) grows one level: the top level is now the MODE ROW (STD/DRADIS/EXT/CHRONO for BSG), each mode node carrying either `children` (a mode WITH its own further nav — STD's console/sub-tab grid, CHRONO's 3-option nav) or none (a mode that IS a single full-screen surface — DRADIS, EXT, both prior-built, [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-INSTANCE]). No new schema field is introduced; [CXC-MENU-SCHEMA]'s existing `label` + optional `children` + optional `annotation` fully expresses a mode node.
- change: "The menu-indexed screen resolver's tree now carries the MODE level at its top, where [SPEC:CXC-MENU] previously scoped mode selection and mode-surface resolution OUT of the tree entirely."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "[CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE] (ratified, [SPEC:CXC-MENU]): the resolver instance's tree top level is STD's 7 consoles; DRADIS/EXT/CHRONO are NOT tree nodes at all — mode selection and mode-surface resolution are named as 'a DIFFERENT, SHALLOWER tier this resolver instance does NOT model', explicitly out of scope."
    reasoning: "The mode-fold question was explicitly asked in the design session's opening turn ('now or later?') and NOT answered there — audit round 1 surfaced this as Q1, the audit's own headline finding. Folding mode in AT THE TOP, above STD's existing grid, lets [CXC-MENU-PATH]'s N-level ancestor-slug join keep working unmodified for STD (see the worked instance below) and gives DRADIS/EXT/CHRONO a place in the SAME tree for label/vocabulary purposes ([CXC-MODE-NAV-RESOLVER-SEAM]) without requiring every mode to resolve via directory-join."
    rejected_alternative: "Keep [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE]'s scope exactly as ratified — mode selection modeled nowhere in any tree, a second, separate ad hoc structure for the mode row and CHRONO's nav (i.e., answer Q1 'later'). This was live until audit round 1; David's ratify closed it in favor of NOW."
    counter_argument: "[CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE]'s scoping-out was a considered, ratified correction (its own `corrected` note records an EARLIER draft that got this wrong the same way) — reopening it risks the same mistake: mode-SURFACE files (`<mode>_mode.svg`) do not fit the directory-join formula."
    counter_response: "This spec does not fold mode-surface resolution into directory-join. DRADIS and EXT — the two modes with no nav of their own, and PRIOR-BUILT worked examples running on (a private host name, not published) (audit round 1, C1) — are LEAVES of the new top level and carry an `annotation.path` override pointing at their existing suffix-named surface file ([CXC-MENU-ANNOTATION]'s designed escape hatch, [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-INSTANCE]); only STD (already directory-join-shaped) and CHRONO (3 directory-join-shaped leaves, not a suffix file) resolve leaves by the ordinary derivation. No node in this tree derives a suffix-named path from the join formula."
    invalidating_assumption: "That every mode which gains its OWN nav in the future (beyond CHRONO) resolves its leaves by directory-join rather than by its own suffix convention. If a future mode's sub-nav needs a non-directory-join path shape, that mode's leaves would need their own `annotation.path` overrides too, same as DRADIS/EXT today — the tree shape does not change, only how many leaves carry an override."
    implication: "[CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE]'s ratified STD-only scope is NOT repealed for the ENGINE (sections 2-4 of [SPEC:CXC-MENU] are untouched); its INSTANCE section is superseded by this document's [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-INSTANCE], which restates STD's binding at one level deeper (mode folded in) with byte-identical derived paths (worked below)."
- status: decided — RATIFIED, audit round 1, Q1 (`docs/architecture/AUDIT-cic-mode-nav.md`, 2026-08-18). The architect's opening-turn question ("now or later?") was asked and unanswered in the original design session; David's audit ratify closes it explicitly: **"YES"** to folding the mode row into `cic_menu` as the top tree level NOW.
- on-ratify: "the amendment to [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE]'s STD-only scope ([SPEC:CXC-MENU]) is now DECIDED by this spec's own audit round, mirroring the two-gate discipline [CXC-MENU-WALK] itself used for its own amendment to [CXC-COMP-MISSING]: gate one (David's ratify) is CLEAR. A second gate — whether the Relationships section's edge to [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE] upgrades from `RELATED_TO` to a typed `AMENDS` edge — stays open exactly as [CXC-MENU-WALK] left it, pending the SAME `EDGETYPE-AMENDS` profile registration neither document has yet."
- see-also: [CXC-MENU-SCHEMA], [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE], [CXC-MENU-ANNOTATION], [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-INSTANCE], [CXC-MODE-NAV-AUDIT-R1]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MODE-NAV]

### [CXC-MODE-NAV-UNIFORM-LEAF] Every nav level resolves uniformly as a `screen` leaf — no `select` node-kind

- definition: [CXC-MENU-KINDS]'s closed, opaque three-token vocabulary (`bespoke-svg` / `pure-widget-no-file` / `live-drop-in`) is NOT extended with a fourth "select" or "nav" token. A node that represents a CHOICE among options (the mode row, CHRONO's 3-option nav) is modeled as a tree node WITH `children`, not as a leaf annotated with a new kind — [CXC-MENU-SCHEMA]'s existing rule (presence of `children` marks a non-leaf) already expresses this with zero new vocabulary. Every LEAF, whatever nav level it terminates, resolves through the SAME [CXC-MENU-RESOLVE] and is one of the three EXISTING kinds.
- requirement: an app author MUST NOT invent a `kind: select` or equivalent; a choice point is expressed structurally (a node with `children`), and the SELECTION mechanics that drive which child is active live in the `selection` primitive ([CXC-MODE-NAV-SELECTION]), entirely OUTSIDE [CXC-MENU-KINDS]'s vocabulary, which governs only whether a LEAF's own art file is expected.
- worked-instance: CHRONO's 3 options (SHIP TIME / JUMP COUNT / TIMER) are three ORDINARY LEAVES under the CHRONO mode node, each defaulting to `kind: bespoke-svg` like any other leaf — nothing about "these are the 3 choices of a selector" is expressed in [CXC-MENU-KINDS] terms; it is expressed by CHRONO having `children` and by the selector widget mounted for CHRONO's nav (an extended [CXC-CIC-TABROW] instance, [CXC-MODE-NAV-SELECTOR-SKIN]) reading its option list FROM those children's labels ([CXC-MODE-NAV-RESOLVER-SEAM]).
- see-also: [CXC-MENU-KINDS], [CXC-MENU-SCHEMA], [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-CHRONO-INSTANCE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MODE-NAV]

### [CXC-MODE-NAV-RESOLVER-SEAM] The resolver never touches selection mechanics; `cic_menu` feeds both sides

- definition: [CXC-MENU-RESOLVE] is UNCHANGED by this spec — it still resolves a LEAF to a `bus://` asset reference and knows nothing about selections, publishing, or namespaces. The mode-nav tree ([CXC-MODE-NAV-TREE]) is the ONE structure that feeds BOTH consumers: a SELECTOR widget at a given nav level (an extended [CXC-CIC-TABROW] instance) reads its LABEL SET (and hence its option count N) from that level's `children` in the tree, exactly as [CXC-CIC-TABROW] already reads `row3`'s five labels from the selected console's sub-tabs; the RESOLVER reads the SAME tree to turn the (mode, ...selections..., leaf) path the selections landed on into an asset reference. Neither consumer duplicates the other's structure.
- requirement: a selector widget mounted for a given nav level MUST derive its option set from that level's tree node's `children` labels ([CXC-MENU-SCHEMA]) and MUST NOT hand-maintain a separate label list (the [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE] `measured-evidence-for-single-source` drift this spec's own tree inherits the discipline against); the resolver MUST continue to be invoked only with a fully-resolved (mode, ...) leaf path — it MUST NOT read a selection namespace itself, and a selection namespace MUST NOT carry an asset path — the two stay separate concerns joined only by the SAME tree.
- see-also: [CXC-MENU-RESOLVE], [CXC-MENU-SCHEMA], [CXC-CIC-TABROW], [CXC-MODE-NAV-TREE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MODE-NAV]

