## 6. BSG instance #1 — the 4-mode top level, and CHRONO's own nav

### [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-INSTANCE] BSG's 4-mode top level: STD (children), DRADIS/EXT (prior-built override leaves), CHRONO (children)

- definition: BSG's Colonial Console binds [CXC-MODE-NAV-TREE]'s shape concretely. The instance's `root` moves ONE level up from [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE]'s STD-scoped `root` — from `compose_projects/bsg/resources/screen_base_layout/std` to `compose_projects/bsg/resources/screen_base_layout` — so the new top level (the 4 modes) can derive `std` as an ordinary slug rather than folding it into `root`. `extension: svg`, `bus-namespace: cic` are unchanged. The top level carries exactly 4 nodes:
  - `STD` (slug `std`) — HAS `children`: [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE]'s existing 7-console x 5-subtab tree, unmodified, now nested one level deeper. Its leaves' derived paths are BYTE-IDENTICAL to today's — `<new-root>/std/<console>/<subtab>.svg` equals `<old-root>/<console>/<subtab>.svg` exactly, because `<new-root>/std` equals `<old-root>`. No asset moves.
  - `DRADIS` (slug `dradis`) — a LEAF, no `children` (DRADIS is one full-screen surface, [CXC-CIC-RADIO-MODE]). Carries `annotation: {path: "compose_projects/bsg/resources/screen_base_layout/dradis_mode.svg"}` — [CXC-MENU-ANNOTATION]'s override, because the mode-surface file follows the `<slug>_mode.svg` suffix convention, not the directory-join [CXC-MENU-PATH] would otherwise derive (`<root>/dradis.svg`, wrong).
  - `EXT` (slug `ext`) — a LEAF, same shape as DRADIS: `annotation: {path: "compose_projects/bsg/resources/screen_base_layout/ext_mode.svg"}`, matching the existing on-disk `ext_mode.svg` [CXC-CIC-RADIO-MODE] already cites.
  - `CHRONO` (slug `chrono`) — HAS `children`: the 3-option nav this design session adds, detailed in [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-CHRONO-INSTANCE].
- provenance-correction: (audit round 1, C1) the original draft of this node treated DRADIS/EXT's override-leaf treatment as this spec's own structural invention, load-bearing on the unresolved Q1. **David's ratify: "I didn't mention DRADIS or EXT because they have been built and are running as worked examples on (a private host name, not published) (as you could see in the screenshot)."** DRADIS and EXT are therefore PRIOR-BUILT, ALREADY-RUNNING worked examples this spec documents and reconciles with the new tree shape (via [CXC-MENU-ANNOTATION]'s override), not screens this design session invented or that wait on Q1 — Q1 is separately, independently RATIFIED (audit round 1) regardless.
- requirement: an app onboarding a mode WITH its own further nav (STD, CHRONO) MUST give that mode node `children` and let [CXC-MENU-PATH] derive its leaves' paths ordinarily; an app onboarding a mode with NO further nav (a single full-screen override, DRADIS/EXT) MUST give that mode node an `annotation.path` override rather than `children` — the choice is mechanical, not a judgment call, once the mode's own asset-naming convention (directory-join vs. suffix) is known.
- measured-hygiene-flag: two candidate CHRONO mode-surface files already exist on disk with inconsistent spelling — `chrono_mode.svg` and `crono_mode.svg` (`compose_projects/bsg/resources/screen_base_layout/`). Now that CHRONO has `children` rather than being a single override leaf, these files may represent CHRONO's persistent chrome (analogous to STD's `colonial_base_ux.svg` base, sitting outside this tree entirely) rather than a resolvable leaf or override path — genuinely unconfirmed, flagged as a build-time hygiene item for whoever wires CHRONO's chrome, not a design fork.
- see-also: [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE], [CXC-MENU-ANNOTATION], [CXC-MENU-PATH], [CXC-CIC-RADIO-MODE], [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-CHRONO-INSTANCE], [CXC-MODE-NAV-AUDIT-R1]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MODE-NAV]

### [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-CHRONO-INSTANCE] CHRONO's 3-option nav — SHIP TIME / JUMP COUNT / TIMER, one already-built `colonial_clock` widget

- definition: CHRONO is the first worked instance of a mode WITH its own nav beneath it (besides STD). Its `children` are 3 LEAVES — label `SHIP TIME` (slug `ship-time`), label `JUMP COUNT` (slug `jump-count`), label `TIMER` (slug `timer`) — each an ORDINARY leaf ([CXC-MODE-NAV-UNIFORM-LEAF]), each deriving its own path by the standard join: `<root>/chrono/ship-time.svg`, `<root>/chrono/jump-count.svg`, `<root>/chrono/timer.svg`. A selector widget (an extended [CXC-CIC-TABROW] instance, [CXC-MODE-NAV-SELECTOR-SKIN]) mounted for CHRONO's nav publishes the selection to `colonial_console/<station>/chrono` ([CXC-MODE-NAV-STATION-SCOPE]) and reads its 3 option labels from these children.
- label-vs-slug: (audit round 1, Q3) the ON-SCREEN label and the derived slug are DELIBERATELY different strings, and this is NOT a new mechanism — it is [CXC-MENU-SLUG]'s existing rule (lowercase, collapse non-alphanumeric runs to a hyphen) applied exactly as written. David: *"perfect. The on-screen labels should not have hyphens should be all caps and be slightly more brief, e.g. 'SHIP TIME' / 'JUMP COUNT' / 'TIMER'. file names should have hyphens, of course."* (audit round 1, Q3, 2026-08-18) — `SHIP TIME` -> `ship-time`, `JUMP COUNT` -> `jump-count`, `TIMER` -> `timer` fall out of [CXC-MENU-SLUG]'s rule with no per-node override needed, the same way [CXC-MENU-CIC-INVENTORY]'s worked table derives BSG's other 35 slugs from their console/sub-tab labels.
- shared-art-default: (duplication, the PRIMARY behavior) needs NO sharing mechanism at all — each of the 3 leaves gets its OWN physical copy of the ship-clock SVG at its own derived path, per David's own preference for the simple path over a special case.
- shared-art-optional: (no new mechanism needed either way) if David later prefers ONE physical file instead of 3 duplicated copies, [SPEC:CXC-MENU] already has two ways to express that with ZERO new engine work — (a) give two of the three leaves an `annotation.path` override pointing at the third leaf's file ([CXC-MENU-ANNOTATION]), or (b) author `<root>/chrono/default.svg` and delete the per-option files, letting [CXC-MENU-WALK]'s ratified ancestor-default walk resolve all 3 to it. Neither is chosen here — this is an availability note, not a decision, and needs no fork because David's own words already settle the DEFAULT (duplicate; it's fine).
- dropped-elements: DONE, not a future MUST (audit round 1, C3 — corrected). The original draft asserted that any baked-in `slot:button@ship-time` / `@jump-countdown` / `@timer` elements in CHRONO's screen art "MUST be dropped" as a forward-looking requirement. David: *"I was vague, because I have not worked through enough of Colonial UX to have a full inventory of my UX element needs. We should not guess at what we may need in the future... It was an architect inference, and I have removed them."* (audit round 1, C3, 2026-08-18) — this node now documents the DONE state (the per-option buttons are already removed; selection is already owned by the mounted selector widget) rather than mandating a future removal, and this spec does NOT extrapolate a general "never bake in per-screen controls" rule from it — David explicitly flagged the inventory of future UX element needs as unsettled and not to be guessed at.
- clock-widget-slot: each CHRONO leaf's clock display is a live slot named `slot:colonial_clock@<binding>`, conforming to [CXC-COMP-SLOT]'s ratified `slot:<widget>@<binding>` grammar ([DEC-CXC-COMP-SLOT-DELIMITER-AT]) — `colonial_clock` is the `<widget>` token (underscore, matching [CXC-COMP-WIDGET-KIND]'s existing underscore-bearing tokens like `tab_row`/`led_bar`).
- widget-provenance: (audit round 1, C4 — corrected) the original draft treated `colonial_clock` as an architect-introduced placeholder token, unverified against David's own words. David: *"I did suggest that we rename the 7 segment widget to 'colonial_clock' earlier this session and it was built — it's running on my glass right now."* (audit round 1, C4, 2026-08-18) — `colonial_clock` is therefore DAVID-ORIGINATED (via the rename request) and ALREADY BUILT AND LIVE, not an unverified architect invention — the rename itself happened earlier in the same session, outside the 5-turn `cic-mode-nav` bracket the extractor scoped to, which is why the original draft could not see its provenance. What remains open is narrower and stays flagged: whether `colonial_clock` is formally REGISTERED as a [CXC-COMP-WIDGET-KIND] interaction kind (vs. expressed as a style variant of an existing kind) — see [DD-MODE-NAV-CLOCK-WIDGET-REGISTRATION], still open, render-engineer territory.
- see-also: [CXC-MENU-ANNOTATION], [CXC-MENU-WALK], [CXC-MENU-SLUG], [CXC-COMP-SLOT], [CXC-COMP-WIDGET-KIND], [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-INSTANCE], [DD-MODE-NAV-CLOCK-WIDGET-REGISTRATION], [CXC-MODE-NAV-AUDIT-R1]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MODE-NAV]

